      "sourceAnchor": "src-p3",
      "wordCount": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "u000004",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "What if the virtues that make us trustworthy are precisely what make us easy to use?",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p5",
      "wordCount": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "u000005",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "A PHILOSOPHICAL ESSAY",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p6",
      "wordCount": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "u000006",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Reading Note",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p8",
      "wordCount": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "u000007",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "Someone begins to suspect that the things through which they learned to be trustworthy also make them easy to use. They follow that suspicion into family, school, work, faith, and biology. Then they themselves receive the power to choose people. The question no longer concerns only the world they observe.",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p9",
      "wordCount": 50
    },
    {
      "id": "u000008",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "The narrator is a literary character. The memories, dialogues, Mara, candidates, and unnamed organization are fictional constructions, not autobiographical testimony or descriptions of identifiable people or institutions. Hypotheses and thought experiments explore possibilities rather than present them as demonstrated frequencies.",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p10",
      "wordCount": 40
    },
    {
      "id": "u000009",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "The philosophical texts and research cited are real. References in the monologue lead to the final bibliography. Empirical findings are presented within limits relevant to the argument: association alone does not establish causation, a model does not guarantee a social outcome, and an example of exploitation does not prove all morality fraudulent. The source selection supports a narrative essay; it is not a systematic review of the entire literature.",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p11",
      "wordCount": 69
    },
    {
      "id": "u000010",
      "chapterId": "front-matter",
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "The accusation remains open. So does the question of whether the narrator seeks truth, a defense against disappointment, or permission to join those who know how to use truth.",
      "sourceAnchor": "src-p12",
      "wordCount": 29
    },
    {
